"""auto-sec-detect - auto_sec's login alerts: journal reader, sender, proof of life.

Installed by role 08 as /usr/local/sbin/auto-sec-detect (root, 0700), only
with detection_enabled: true. Python standard library only. Alert only:
it never ends a session, bans an address or locks an account.

Two sensors write small event files into the root-only queue
/var/spool/auto-sec/events:
  A  the login hook (/usr/local/sbin/auto-sec-login-hook, run by PAM)
  B  `auto-sec-detect watch`, which follows the journal

Commands (each one is a systemd unit of its own):
  watch          sensor B (auto-sec-login-watch.service). It reads only
                 lines whose program path the journal itself recorded
                 (_EXE: sshd, login, su, sudo), plus Webmin's, and only
                 lines that match a strict pattern.
  send           the sender (auto-sec-alert.service). Joins the two
                 sensors' events for one login, applies the quiet list,
                 bundles bursts and sends one Telegram message.
  alive          asks the sender for the daily "still alive" message
                 (auto-sec-alive.timer).
  check          the independent checker (auto-sec-check.timer, every 5
                 minutes). Sends its own message when something is wrong,
                 and again every hour until it is fixed.
  stopped UNIT   says that a detection unit stopped or failed
                 (auto-sec-notify@.service).
  test           sends a test message now and prints its id.
  goodbye        says that an auto_sec run takes login alerts out.
  status [--json]   what runs, the last event and message, the queue.

Never quiet: a console login, a root SSH login without a quiet key, a
root login through su, sudo, Webmin or a door auto_sec does not know.

Limits, said plainly: if Telegram cannot be reached, nothing here can
tell you. Whoever is root on this server can stop every one of these
units, or change this file. This program guards against a login nobody
was told about, not against a careful intruder who already is root.

Trust: event files can only be written by root (the queue is 0700). The
values inside still come from PAM and from log text that a client can
partly shape (a user name, a reverse DNS name), so every value is cut
to printable characters and a fixed length before it is used or sent. No
value is ever run, and no message holds the token, a password or a
command's arguments.

--root DIR is for the tests only (tests/detection): every path is then
taken under DIR, and systemctl/journalctl from DIR/stub.
